    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <LessonHeader 
                LessonNumber="9"
                Title="Asynchronous Messaging Patterns"
                Duration="12 min"
                Level="Advanced" />
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Sync vs Async Communication</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Synchronous (HTTP/gRPC)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Request-Response</li>
                                <li>Caller waits for response</li>
                                <li>Tight coupling</li>
                                <li>Chain failures</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Asynchronous (Messaging)</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Fire and forget</li>
                                <li>Caller continues immediately</li>
                                <li>Loose coupling</li>
                                <li>Resilient to failures</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Messaging Patterns</h2>
                    <p>Short summary of point-to-point and pub/sub messaging patterns and when to use each (commands vs events).</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">Point-to-Point (Queue):
- One producer, one consumer
- Each message processed once
- Good for commands

Publish-Subscribe (Topic):
- One producer, many consumers
- Each consumer gets a copy
- Good for events</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Event Types</h2>
                    <p>Examples of domain events, integration events, and commands used to decouple microservices.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Domain Event - something happened
public record OrderPlaced(Guid OrderId, Guid CustomerId, decimal Total);

// Integration Event - cross-service communication
public record OrderPlacedIntegrationEvent
{
    public Guid OrderId { get; init; }
    public Guid CustomerId { get; init; }
    public decimal Total { get; init; }
    public DateTime OccurredAt { get; init; }
}

// Command - request to do something
public record ReserveInventory(Guid OrderId, List&lt;OrderItem&gt; Items);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Message Brokers</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>RabbitMQ:</strong> Traditional message broker, flexible routing</li>
                        <li><strong>Azure Service Bus:</strong> Enterprise-grade, managed</li>
                        <li><strong>Apache Kafka:</strong> Event streaming, high throughput</li>
                        <li><strong>Amazon SQS/SNS:</strong> AWS managed queues/topics</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Async messaging enables loose coupling.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use events for things that happened.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use commands for requests to do something.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
